writing a one punch man workout was  always going to be a little different  from writing other workouts inspired by  fictional characters  the obvious reason for this is that we  already know saitama's entire training  his workout infamously consists of 100  push-ups  100 setups  100 squats and 10 kilometers of running  the joke is that this simple routine was  able to get him to the point where he  could jump from the moon to the earth  and  punch giant meteorites into dust it's  just goofy fun  so what more is there to say first i  want to address this workout as it's  written  to see what merits it actually has  secondly i want to create a revised  version that will offer real benefits  and it turns out that this workout  even as written may be more effective  than you would think  the first thing to address is that  saitama's program is pretty incomplete  for most purposes  is that one set of 100 push-ups or is it  10 sets of 10  what order are we completing these in  and where is the progressive overload  here  what happens when 100 push-ups becomes  really easy the one punch man workout is  also a little imbalanced  there are no pulling movements  whatsoever let alone twisting or  extension  and you're likely to find that 100  sit-ups is a lot easier than 100  push-ups  and much easier than a 10 km run  going that far every single day is going  to either take a considerable amount of  time  or be quite hard on the old joints still  there are ways around this and there are  mitigating factors  so let's take a look at the good stuff  for a moment i recently posted an  article over at thebioneer.com called  the surprising benefits of doing 100  push-ups a day or more  i'll be repeating some of that here but  check it out if you want some more  background  i'm assuming here that what saitama  actually meant was that he would  complete a single set of 100 push-ups  and that this should be tough doing this  has surprising benefits  firstly if you're going to failure or  close to failure then you'll be  recruiting  larger faster twitch muscle fibers even  with this relatively low amount of  weight  that's because all the slow twitch  muscle fibers will fatigue and give up  leaving only the more powerful motor  units to keep you going  this in turn means you can develop  strength through high rep calisthenics  despite what you may think in short your  91st  rep of your 100 rep max feels and acts  similar to lifting 91 of your one rep  max  not exactly the same mind but it will  have many of the same benefits  i took a 10 month hiatus from bench  press during the current pandemic  and returned for the first time last  week i decided to ease myself back into  it but i was pretty comfortable  performing five reps of 125 kilograms  and that was with incomplete recovery  from my previous set  because that's how i roll point is high  rep push-ups and some calisthenics  variations  were enough to let me maintain most of  my strength  better yet is that this type of training  builds strength endurance  this is something i've mentioned in the  past but i believe it is severely  overlooked  point is it's not all that useful being  able to lift something really heavy  just once far more useful is being able  to lift something heavy  lots of times or hold it in place for a  long time i don't get why more people  don't focus on this aspect of their  performance  and it's something i think crossfit has  gotten very right the anecdote i  always come back to is tom platts who  would do a hundred plus reps on the leg  press every single day  to build his massive legs he could  eventually perform a hundred reps of  squats  with 225 pounds on his back that's  extremely insane and to me it's one of  the most exciting examples  of an alternative approach to training  far more impressive than  a single extremely heavy squat just give  it a go if you  aren't already impressed with this of  course this method did involve  progressive overload unlike the one  punch man approach  but the point is that he was using high  reps to failure or close  to get there that's what 100 push-ups or  air squats will be for most people  and doing all those squats isn't that  far removed from the kind of training  used by the great gamma  or mike tyson to build a massive  powerful legs either  check out my bane training video for  more on that this strength endurance  comes with a number of other unique  adaptations  what's really interesting is that this  might improve blood supply to the  muscles  this occurs in part through angiogenesis  or capillarization  the formation of new tiny blood vessels  that both carry blood to the muscles  and also take waste products away from  them such as the hydrogen ions  that cause muscle burn and fatigue the  result is not only greater energy supply  during a set but also more efficient  removal of waste  which in turn helps to go for longer  this is amazing for preventing  injuries too because you'll be providing  the tendons with greater blood supply  it also means more recovery between  workouts because getting more blood to  the muscles  helping you to handle the repetitive  grind of doing anything daily like this  so the high rep stuff is good but i feel  that a hundred sit-ups is a little out  of place  and we really need something for pulling  in there i'd recommend using bodyweight  rows to balance out with the push-ups  why because the row more closely matches  the push-up both in terms of the number  of reps you can do  and in terms of the biomechanics one of  the great surprising benefits of push-up  training like this  is that if you're doing them correctly  by not allowing your waist to sag to the  ground  then you'll also be strengthening your  core for greater performance  it's like moving plank especially at  these higher rep ranges  the bodyweight row is perfect for  offering the same stability  in the opposite direction anti-extension  that's because you now need to prevent  your buttocks from touching the ground  i've talked about this in much more  depth in a very recent video so make  sure you check out that if you haven't  seen it already  and if it's punching we want then we  also need to train the transverse plane  the body's ability to twist on the spot  that's why i'd recommend swapping the  sit-up  for at least bicycle crunches or better  yet alternating v-ups returning to the  hollow body position between each rep  adding some training for the obliques  this way not only makes sense for a  one-punch mana workout  but also provides a more complete  midsection development many people will  find that performing  sets of 100 reps is tough on all of  these exercises  if that goes for you then simply aim to  complete the sets in as little time as  possible  that means you'll be aiming to keep the  rest time between sets as small as you  can  this results in an incomplete recovery  turning it into a form of metabolic  conditioning  in short it still helps you to get  better at removing metabolites and keeps  your heart rate up  it might take you an hour to do 100  push-ups the first day but in a few  months you'll be bashing out sets of 100  under a minute the adaptations here come  fast  if you can't do a single push-up or  squat then you can make the movement  easier you can perform squat pulses  shout out to the awesome jax blade who  covered this topic in his saitama video  or you can use push-ups on your knees  complete as many reps using proper form  as you can and then drop immediately to  the easier variation  this is called a mechanical drop set and  i'm all about that  and if you're facing the other problem  and you find that this is all too easy  then you can use the same strategy in  reverse do explosive push-ups or  weighted push-ups  then drop to the normal variation  remember the benefits of doing high-rep  calisthenics  come partly from going to or near  failure  those not interested in performing the  one punch man workout but keen to get  the benefits  can try using long drop sets in their  training or incorporating flush sets  a flush set means that you might end  your chest workout with 100 reps on the  chest press  which is a fantastic way to create an  immense burn while also actually  enhancing recovery  but we can't talk about saitama's  training without addressing the running  portion  as i mentioned the 10 km run is a little  out of place in this equation  given that it's a lot harder when  performed daily or at least it is if you  go fast  take it much slower by which i mean  maybe running it in one hour and 30  minutes or even longer  and it shouldn't be too hard on the  bones and joints nor should it result in  too much burnout  this is a big mistake that a lot of  people make with their cardio  throwing in junk miles at something just  below their anaerobic threshold and  weighing themselves down over time  in his book beyond training ben  greenfield discusses how this can do  more harm than good  the irony is that humans are meant to  run long distances  some theories suggest that this is the  main way we would have survived in the  wild  and indigenous tribes like the tara  hamara seem to support that notion  long time viewers know that these are  the guys who run up to 435 miles over  two days  the difference is that they would run  those distances very slowly just as i  described  running is not just about cardio  performance it's about form and muscle  tone  don't go as fast as you physically can  to begin with just enjoy it and aim to  complete the distance in any time  you can even walk and this will actually  enhance recovery  by improving circulation not to mention  the obvious calorie burn benefits  that said if this is taking you all day  then i'm sure no one will judge you for  sticking to 5 km  and as you do run focus on your form  relax the shoulders keep the joints  stacked  ideally run in minimal footwear and use  a four foot stride  keep the core tight and don't let  yourself lean forwards or back too much  there are many more benefits you get  from mindful gentle running and walking  in fact i made a series of guided runs  and walks for a company called ifit  there i discuss these benefits and talk  about how you can focus more on your  form as you go  i'm showing clips from the trailer up on  the screen right now you can check that  out if you're interested in this stuff  and there's a three month trial i think  being mindful of your form and  maintaining this throughout your runs  will make everything tighter and  stronger  and it will help you to perform better  when fatigued if you feel pain in your  feet  knees or back then stop assess your form  and come back to it  if you're looking for an alternative  that will bring some of the same  benefits  look for something like the 100 up this  movement involves jumping from one foot  to another  to improve your hip stability as though  running on the spot but bringing these  up high  this is also a great way to check your  form and improve your form when fatigue  i'll make a video on this topic alone in  future but some claim it could  completely transform your running game  g walter george who invented the  technique apparently used to go from an  inexperienced runner to one of the best  of his time  this was during the 1800s so there you  go  that's my take on why so many people  have seen some pretty decent results  from the one punch man workout switching  to such high reps is a frightening  prospect for bodybuilders and  powerlifters  used to sets of 12 and 4 respectively  but it brings its own rewards  rewards that don't necessarily translate  to bigger numbers in the gym  which can be extremely beneficial in  life and in competition  and can result in a pretty mean physique  i'm not saying do this instead i'm just  saying  maybe consider those higher reps the  other really appealing thing about this  type of training of course is just that  it's so  simple if you've tried to follow complex  training programs in the past  and found yourself getting muddled and  confused then the idea of just picking  four exercises and repeating them every  single day is probably really appealing  almost any exercise regime that's that  simple is going to leave  some things out this can be a great way  for somebody new to training to get  started and just to get into the habit  of doing something daily  building good habits and then they can  build on that  as they start to see their basic  strength and  conditioning improve if you want to  learn more about performing high rep  calisthenics  then i actually have a free training  program you can get from the link in the  description  that's the bodyweight max stimulus  program  if you want something more in depth that  covers every aspect of strength and  performance  that improves strength mobility running  speed jump height reception brain  function and more  then of course you can check out my full  ebook and training program  super functional training that's on  discount right now during the pandemic  and it explains all of this in much more  detail finally if you want to learn all  about functional training and how it  applies to your real life or sports and  athletics  where it came from then you can  pre-order my physical book  functional training and beyond that will  be released early next year and it's  available at a discounted price if you  pre-order now  i hope you enjoyed this video guys and  found it useful and interesting if you  did then please consider leaving a like  and sharing it around  that helps me out so much thank you so  much for all your support this year  it's actually been a crazy year for the  bioneer and i also hope you all have a  fantastic christmas or  holiday season and that 2021 is a better  year for everyone  it can't be worse right thanks a ton for  watching guys  and bye for now

